WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.940
Film critics might provide an invaluable filter
between audiences and bad movies, but they

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.860
don't always get it right.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:11.799
In the case of these secretly amazing movies,
most reviewers majorly missed the mark.

00:00:11.799 --> 00:00:15.929
For those that put stock in the Rotten Tomatoes
score before ponying up ticket fare, these

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:18.560
are the movies you might've missed the first
time around.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.419
And be warned, there are spoilers ahead.

00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:22.419
The Cell

00:00:22.419 --> 00:00:26.369
Directed by Tarsem Singh, The Cell is not
a movie for everyone.

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:30.079
But critics who accused the movie of being
shallow and derivative might have been a bit

00:00:30.079 --> 00:00:31.800
too harsh in retrospect.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.370
The film tells the story of Catherine Deane,
a child psychologist who uses some impressive

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:41.420
tech to enter the subconscious of a comatose
boy, hoping to bring him back into the real

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.420
world.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.579
Thanks to her unique set of skills, she's
asked by an FBI agent to explore the mind

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:52.199
of an unconscious MAN who'd imprisoned a girl
in a bizarre death trap before having a seizure.

00:00:52.199 --> 00:00:57.460
Now, she only has hours left to live, so Deane
is tasked with finding her whereabouts.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:02.020
But this is easier said than done, since his
subconscious is a nightmare world of torture

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.930
devices, horned monsters, and living dolls
that resemble his victims.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:11.470
It's an S&amp;M fever dream where corpses are
bathed in blood, horses are dissected with

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:16.290
glass slides, and men have their intestines
slowly pulled from their bodies.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:21.080
So, The Cell is full of depravity, but it's
still pretty gorgeous to look at, thanks to

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.330
some horrific tributes to artists like Damien
Hirst, Odd Nerdrum, and H.R. Giger.

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:31.810
And the costume game is at its finest with
designer Eiko Ishioka's array of jumpsuits,

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:35.310
demonic purple wings, and sadistic sci-fi
masks.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.310
Better still, the sets are practical, the
performances are on point, and the result

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:42.900
is something big, bloody, and perversely beautiful.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.030
Of course, they didn't all get it wrong.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:48.500
Roger Ebert was one of the few critics who
actually liked it and in fact declared it

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.330
was "one of the best films of the year."

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:52.330
The Hunted

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:56.790
When it comes to nail-biters, William Friedkin
is one of the best in the business.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.710
After all, he's the guy who made films like
The French Connection and The Exorcist.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:05.950
In The Hunted, he gives us a chase movie for
the ages, even if critics weren't too impressed.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.500
The story follows a tired tracker named L.T.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:13.530
Bonham who used to teach Special Ops soldiers,
but unfortunately, his training was a little

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:14.569
too good.

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:19.700
After seeing some pretty horrible things overseas,
an old pupil has lost his mind and now spends

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.870
his time picking off deer hunters.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.969
So Bonham is brought in to give his student
one last lesson.

00:02:25.969 --> 00:02:30.719
Tommy Lee Jones is amazing as the weary survivalist,
a man who knows what he has to do, but that

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.380
doesn't mean he has to enjoy it.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.300
"This yours?"

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:36.640
"What the…"

00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:42.349
As for Benecio Del Toro, he's both scary and
sympathetic as a man who's seen and spilled

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:43.980
far too much blood.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.971
Friedkin expertly follows these two as they
chase each other down, and when it comes down

00:02:47.971 --> 00:02:51.450
to the final showdown, there are no showy
ninja moves here.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:56.230
It's painful, brutal, and in your face, which
pretty much sums up the entire feel of this

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.519
underrated thriller.

00:02:58.519 --> 00:02:59.730
Constantine

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:04.569
When Constantine was released in 2005, it
had a devil of a time with moviegoers.

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:09.489
It lost money at the domestic box office,
and critics did their best to exorcise the

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:10.730
film from theaters.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:15.109
But those reviewers must've been in league
with Lucifer because Constantine is one hell

00:03:15.109 --> 00:03:16.110
of a movie.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:19.989
Granted, it doesn't have much in common with
Hellblazer—the comic it's loosely based

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:25.129
on—but nevertheless, it's an amazingly fun
film noir about a chain-smoking cynic who

00:03:25.129 --> 00:03:27.900
deports demons for entirely selfish reasons.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.999
As a kid, he tried to take his own life, and
now he's damned for all eternity.

00:03:32.999 --> 00:03:37.700
So his plan is to exorcise his way to heaven,
and he finally gets a chance at saving his

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.389
soul when a cop asks him to take on a strange
case.

00:03:41.389 --> 00:03:45.819
Directed by Francis Lawrence, Constantine
plunges our hero into a world where otherworldly

00:03:45.819 --> 00:03:51.459
beings spend their evenings at a supernatural
club, and heroes blast demons with a crucifix

00:03:51.459 --> 00:03:52.459
shotguns.

00:03:52.459 --> 00:03:56.840
In this freaky film, cats can guide you to
the underworld, holy water is stored in five-gallon

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:00.810
jugs, and angels dress to the nines in killer
pinstripe suits.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:04.319
John Constantine is one of Reeves' best performances.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:09.010
The man is playing a mash-up between Sam Spade
and Neo from The Matrix, expertly blending

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:14.459
a snarky sense of humor, detached detective
cool, and secret side of antihero empathy.

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:19.810
Then there's Tilda Swinton as an incredibly
suave Gabriel and Peter Stormare as the sleaziest

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:21.110
Satan of all time.

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:25.230
With all that awesomeness, it's baffling the
movie did so poorly, but to all the critics

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.520
who've hated this film, Constantine has a
little message for you…

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:38.020
Knowing

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:42.380
There's no denying that Nicolas Cage has had
a checkered cinematic career…

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:43.740
"huh HAAAA!"

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.170
...but faithful fans are rewarded every so
often with a legitimately great movie like

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:52.150
Joe, Adaptation, Bad Lieutenant… and Knowing.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:56.630
Though almost every film critic wishes the
last movie would disappear in an extinction-level

00:04:56.630 --> 00:05:02.050
event, Knowing is genuinely thrilling and
poses some interesting philosophical questions.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:06.020
When it's not freaking you out, it's making
you think, which is why Roger Ebert was the

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:10.710
lone holdout who declared it was one of "the
best science-fiction films" he'd ever seen.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:15.430
The plot revolves around a rational professor,
played by Cage, whose son discovers a 50-year-old

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.250
document covered in numbers.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:22.710
Despite his skepticism, Koestler realizes
these numbers are a code predicting the dates

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.470
and body counts of major disasters.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:30.670
And as he digs deeper into the mystery, he
realizes something bad is looming on the horizon.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:36.300
With the unsettling appearance of some otherworldly
strangers, Koestler begins questioning everything

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.660
he's ever known about the universe.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:43.970
With director Alex Proyas at the helm, Knowing
is just brimming with dread, the same creeping

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:46.950
kind of fear you'd find in a movie like Signs.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:52.430
Plus, the film grapples with concepts like
free will versus predestination, a deterministic

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.790
universe versus a random one.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:59.200
You might not like where the movie eventually
sides, but it's a film that takes chances

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.980
and generates ideas that are well worth exploring
after the credits roll.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:04.520
"And you have to do it.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:05.520
You have to.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:06.690
Or I'll fire you.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.760
Do you understand?"

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:09.880
Super

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:13.960
Before directing Guardians of the Galaxy,
James Gunn put his stamp on the superhero

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:19.180
genre with Super, an upbeat version of Taxi
Driver where the supposed heroes take out

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:22.010
drug dealers with pipe bombs and claws.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:26.140
If you can stomach the gore, then you'll find
yourself nervously chuckling along with one

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:29.770
of the best—and nastiest—superhero satires.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:34.510
The plot follows a schlubby cook named Frank
whose wife has just left him for the world's

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.050
sleaziest drug dealer.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:41.220
But after receiving a vision from God—one
involving razor blades and tentacles—Frank

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:46.240
believes it's his divine mission to become
a superhero, fight crime, and rescue his wife.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:50.710
And accompanying him on his quest is a comic
book nerd who has way too much fun breaking

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:52.710
legs and bashing heads.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:57.690
Disguised as the Crimson Bolt, Frank uses
a pipe wrench to punish both child molesters

00:06:57.690 --> 00:06:59.970
and people who cut in line.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:04.970
The violence is shockingly hard to watch,
and as a result, Super feels like we're watching

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:08.190
a schizophrenic madman who's building towards
something terrible.

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:12.470
And it's that over-the-top bloodshed that
angered so many critics.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:14.670
But the violence here is kind of the point.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:18.800
If superheroes existed in real-life, they
wouldn't be the most stable people on the

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:24.200
planet, so Super is a savage and side-splitting
response to every comic book movie to ever

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:28.450
come out of Hollywood… which is kind of
ironic considering what Gunn would later sign

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:29.450
up to direct.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:32.400
"Face the wrath of the Crimson Bolt!"

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:36.800
The Majestic

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:41.090
In between Stephen King adaptations, Frank
Darabont decided to make a movie in the style

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:42.430
of Frank Capra.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:47.140
The result was The Majestic, a film so sweet
and nostalgic that it's shocking to think

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:49.660
the same director would later make The Mist.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:54.680
But while that grisly creature feature is
about as pessimistic as movies get, The Majestic

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:58.890
believes in old-fashioned ideas like right
and wrong and freedom of speech.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:03.910
The story follows a blacklisted screenwriter
named Peter Appleton who get amnesia and winds

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:08.180
up in a sleepy little town where he's mistaken
for a long-lost World War II vet.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:12.610
And since he can't remember who he is or where
he came from, Peter accepts the story and

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:15.710
bonds with his new dad and a wary love interest.

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:20.190
Eventually, Peter's memories come flooding
back, threatening his new existence, and things

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:24.280
get even more complicated when he's called
upon to testify before the House Un-American

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.790
Activities Committee.

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:30.250
But Peter rises to the occasion and delivers
a rousing speech in defense of the right to

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:32.279
say and believe whatever you want.

00:08:32.279 --> 00:08:36.659
Jim Carrey is on the top of his game here,
delivering a dramatic performance that's right

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:40.800
up there with his roles in The Truman Show
and Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind.

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.730
And The Majestic is an inspiring little film,
the kind we hardly see anymore.

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:47.220
Vanilla Sky

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:52.649
The world of Vanilla Sky is filled with Monet
sunsets and Bob Dylan streets, but that doesn't

00:08:52.649 --> 00:08:54.290
mean you'd want to live there.

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:55.290
Why not?

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:59.839
Well, it's also a world of crazy stalkers,
creepy face masks, and a dreadful feeling

00:08:59.839 --> 00:09:02.430
that something awful is waiting in the dark.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:07.620
Based on the 1998 Spanish film Open Your Eyes,
Cameron Crowe's fifth feature marks the spot

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.079
where many believe he started losing his touch.

00:09:10.079 --> 00:09:14.980
Despite its reputation, this Tom Cruise film
is a massive mindbender with more layers than

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:17.339
Inception and more twists than Memento.

00:09:17.339 --> 00:09:21.800
And Cruise is really earning his paycheck
here as David Ames, an ultra-rich playboy

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:26.320
who has his life ripped apart and then starts
losing his mind…maybe.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:30.639
The superstar really sells David's growing
fear and paranoia, and his relationships with

00:09:30.639 --> 00:09:35.269
the women of the film are masterfully crafted,
highlighting two very different sides of the

00:09:35.269 --> 00:09:36.269
same man.

00:09:36.269 --> 00:09:41.420
The movie is also filled with some pretty
horrific images and genuinely disturbing moments,

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:45.949
from bizarre bedroom body-swaps to grotesque
shots of Cruise's face.

00:09:45.949 --> 00:09:50.070
There's also a fantastic soundtrack that features
one of the most disturbing uses of a Beach

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:52.620
Boys song you'll ever hear.

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:53.620
The Book of Eli

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:58.689
This movie starts off with Denzel Washington
shooting and eating a feral housecat... and

00:09:58.689 --> 00:10:00.810
it only gets crazier from there.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:05.459
Set in a post-apocalyptic world, The Book
of Eli takes place in a time when moist towelettes

00:10:05.459 --> 00:10:10.370
are used as currency, people pay to charge
their iPods, and the local saloons make their

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:11.779
money selling water.

00:10:11.779 --> 00:10:16.819
It's a dusty and depressing realm where those
who can read hold all the power, and faith

00:10:16.819 --> 00:10:18.990
is more powerful than a loaded gun.

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:24.690
The sci-fi western follows Washington as Eli,
a holy warrior wandering across what's left

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:26.629
of the United States.

00:10:26.629 --> 00:10:31.260
Accompanied by a machete and Mila Kunis, Eli is carrying the last remaining

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:35.899
copy of the King James Bible, and he hopes
to get the holy book to a safe place on the

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:36.899
coast.

00:10:36.899 --> 00:10:41.360
Unfortunately, a small-town dictator wants
the book for himself, knowing it can help

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.809
him establish his evil empire.

00:10:43.809 --> 00:10:48.680
Only Eli isn't giving up the Good Book so
easily, and instead of turning the other cheek,

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:53.649
he's prepared to take eye for an eye to make
sure the relic makes it safely across America.

00:10:53.649 --> 00:10:58.379
In addition to some masterful action scenes—The
Book of Eli is a powerful commentary on the

00:10:58.379 --> 00:11:02.810
power of religion, but ultimately, it's a
story that's also all about the power of the

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:07.050
written word, and how books can shape entire
civilizations.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:08.050
Thanks for watching!

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:10.490
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.379
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

